{%- doc -%}
  Contrast and palette corrections for the Appstle subscription widget on the product page.

  Appstle paints its own text in a colour that matches this theme's *background* token, cream
  #F4F3E9. Measured on the live product page, that put six of its elements between 1.00 and 1.11
  against the ground behind them - cream on cream, and cream on the white reward rows. They were
  not faint, they were invisible; the subscriber reward tiers in particular were reported by the
  ONA team as unreadable. Two more, its own links, sat at 2.63 in Appstle's brand blue.

  Everything is scoped to `.appstle_sub_widget`, the widget's own root, so nothing here can reach
  the theme's markup. These are the app's class names, not ours: if an Appstle update renames
  them the rules go inert rather than wrong, and `tests/appstle-widget-contrast.test.mjs` records
  what was measured so the next person can re-check rather than guess.
{%- enddoc -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  /* The widget inherits cream where it means ink. Everything that carries text gets the
     foreground token; both grounds it sits on - cream and white - clear AA against it. */
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_loyalty_title,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_tooltip_title,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle-loyalty-table-data,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle-loyalty-billing-cycle,
  /* Appstle's own spelling of "percentage". Kept as they ship it - correcting it here would
     simply stop matching. */
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle-precentage-loyalty {
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }

  /* Appstle renders its own "Purchase options" heading immediately above the theme's. It has
     been invisible all along, by the same accident that hid everything else, so giving the rest
     its colour back would surface a duplicate heading. Hidden deliberately instead of relying on
     cream-on-cream to keep doing it. */
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_widget_title {
    display: none;
  }

  /* White on Appstle's brand blue measures 2.63, and the blue belongs to no part of this
     palette. The foreground token carries white at a far higher ratio and matches the buttons
     beside it. */
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_link,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_subcription_link {
    background-color: var(--color-foreground);
    color: var(--color-background);
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
